course: biology
lesson: 59
title: Seeds and fruit and how they travel
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A seed is a baby plant packed with a lunchbox, and the worst place for it to land is right under its parent, where it would fight for light, water and space, so plants have invented every kind of transport.
explain: Some go by air: blow a dandelion clock and each seed floats off on a tiny parachute, and sycamore helicopters spin down slowly so the wind can carry them away.
explain: Some hitch a ride: burdock burrs hook onto your dog's fur after a walk, birds eat berries and drop the seeds far away, and squirrels bury acorns and forget half of them, planting new oaks by accident.
explain: Some go by sea, like the floating coconut, and some simply explode: on a hot summer day on the heath you can hear gorse pods pop and fling their seeds out.
explain: Cartoons show a tomato as a vegetable and a banana full of big seeds; to a scientist a fruit is the part that grows from the flower and holds the seeds, so a tomato is a fruit, and a shop banana has only tiny dots instead of real seeds.
fact: Each dandelion seed has a parachute of fine hairs that makes a ring of swirling air above it, slowing its fall so the wind can carry it many kilometres.
fact: Sycamore seeds have a wing that makes them spin as they fall, slowing them down; sycamore is a kind of maple, and maple seeds grow in winged pairs.
fact: A coconut is a seed in a fibrous, air-filled husk that floats and keeps sea water out; most other seeds would sink and be killed by the salt, but a coconut can float for months and travel thousands of kilometres across the sea and still sprout.
fact: Birds eat berries, and the seeds pass through unharmed and come out in their droppings far away; some berries are poisonous to people, so never eat wild berries you cannot name.
fact: Burrs are seed heads covered in tiny hooks; George de Mestral invented Velcro in the 1940s after looking at burrs from his dog under a microscope.
fact: Squirrels and jays bury acorns and nuts for winter and forget many, which grow into new trees; one jay can bury several thousand acorns in an autumn.
fact: Gorse, broom and Himalayan balsam have pods that dry and twist on hot dry days until they burst and fling the seeds out, and the sandbox tree flings its seeds at around 250 km per hour.
fact: A fruit is the part of a plant that grows from the flower and holds the seeds, so tomatoes, peppers, cucumbers and pea pods are fruits to a scientist, though cooks call them vegetables; cut an apple across and you find a star of seeds.
fact: Unripe fruits are often green, hard and sour so animals do not eat them before the seeds are ready; ripe ones turn soft, sweet and bright.
fact: The Svalbard Global Seed Vault in Norway stores over a million seed samples deep in a frozen mountain, as a back-up if crops are lost; cold and dryness slow a seed right down, so it stays alive for a very long time.
fact: Some lotus seeds found in a dry lake bed in China sprouted after about 1300 years, protected by a very hard coat; seeds that can wait help a plant survive bad years, sprouting when the drought or cold is over.
fact: Violet seeds have a small fatty bit that ants love; ants carry the seed home, eat the fatty bit and throw the seed onto their rich rubbish pile, where it grows.
fact: Maple syrup comes from the sugary sap of the sugar maple tree in Canada and the USA.
exercise: Why does a dandelion seed float away instead of dropping? | Its parachute of fine hairs slows its fall so the wind carries it | It is filled with a gas lighter than air, like a tiny party balloon | It flaps tiny wings | It is too small for gravity to pull
exercise: Why do sycamore seeds spin as they fall? | Their wing makes them spin, slowing the fall | The tree throws them off its branches with a twist | They are dizzy from falling from such a height | The spin helps them drill deep down into the soil
exercise: How did the coconut on the holiday beach get there? | It floated across the sea in its air-filled husk | A big seabird dropped it from high in the sky | It rolled down from the mountains | Tourists always leave them there
exercise: How do blackbirds help rowan trees? | They eat the berries and drop the seeds far away in their droppings | They plant the berries in the soil with their beaks | They chase away insects that eat the seeds | They build nests that keep the seeds warm all through the long winter
exercise: How do burdock burrs travel on a dog's fur? | Tiny hooks catch on fur and fall off somewhere new | They are glued onto the fur with sticky sap from the stem | The dog carries them in its mouth | They crawl onto the dog by themselves
exercise: How does a squirrel burying acorns help oak trees? | It forgets some, and they grow into new oaks | It waters them every day | It eats the bad acorns only | It buries them right next to the parent tree
exercise: Why do gorse pods pop on a hot summer day? | They dry and twist until they burst and fling the seeds | They are full of a gas that catches fire in the hot sunshine | Insects inside are trying to escape | The heat cooks them like popcorn
exercise: What did you find when you cut an apple across? | A star of seeds, as an apple is a fruit grown from a flower | A hard stone in the middle, because an apple is really a kind of nut | Nothing, apples have no seeds | Roots, because apples grow underground
exercise: Which of these is a fruit to a scientist? | A tomato | A carrot root | A potato tuber | A lettuce leaf
exercise: What is the Svalbard Global Seed Vault for? | Keeping a back-up of seeds in case crops are lost | Growing fresh vegetables under the ice in the Arctic | Selling seeds to tourists | Testing seeds in the snow
exercise: How long did some old lotus seeds from China wait before sprouting? | About 1300 years | About thirteen years | About 130000 years | About thirteen days
exercise: Why do ants carry violet seeds back to their nest? | To eat the small fatty bit on the seed | To build the walls of their nest out of them | To feed the seeds whole to their hungry queen | Because the seeds smell like other ants
exercise: Why does a Japanese maple grow its seeds in pairs with wings? | Each seed has a wing to spin away on the wind | So two trees grow touching | So birds can carry them off in pairs, one in each foot | Wings keep the seeds warm
exercise: What invention came from looking at burrs? | Velcro | The zip | The parachute | The button
exercise: How does a coconut seed survive a long trip at sea? | Its thick husk floats and keeps sea water out of the seed | It drinks the sea water happily | It sinks and rolls slowly along the bottom of the sea until it reaches land | It is carried by dolphins
exercise: Why should you never eat wild berries you cannot name? | Some are poisonous to people | They are all far too sour to swallow | They belong to the birds by law | They make you sneeze for hours afterwards
test: in my cartoon the tomato's in the veg gang. is it a fruit or a veg then? | To a scientist it's a fruit, as it holds seeds; cooks say veg | It is only a vegetable because it is not sweet like the real fruits | It is a root, like a carrot | It is a nut, because it has seeds
test: Why would a plant want its seeds to land far from it? | So the young plants don't fight the parent for light, water and space | So it can get a rest from its children | Because the soil right round the parent plant is always poisonous to seeds | So birds find them more easily
test: How could you test which helicopter seeds fall slowest? | Drop them from the same height and time each, one change at a time | Throw them as hard as you can | Count how many are on the ground under the tree after a windy night | Paint them and see which looks fastest
test: Why would most seeds die if they were carried by sea water? | Salt water gets in and kills them, and they sink | Fish always eat them | They would get too cold in the deep water | Sea water makes them grow far too fast and they burst
test: Why are many unripe fruits green, hard and sour? | So animals don't eat them before the seeds are ready | Because they are still cold | Because they are a different kind of fruit from the ripe ones | So they look like leaves to the Sun
test: Why might an oak tree grow better far from its parent? | It gets more light and doesn't share water and soil with a giant | It misses its parent and grows angry | Acorns only grow near water | Oaks grow better in the deep shade of other, bigger oaks around them
test: Why might gorse pods burst on a hot dry day instead of a wet one? | Dry, windy weather spreads seeds best, and pods twist as they dry | Rain makes them explode too early | The pods are scared of thunder | Hot days make the seeds jump by themselves, like fleas on a dog
test: Why would you keep seeds somewhere very cold and dry? | It slows everything down so they stay alive for a very long time | So they grow faster when they are warmed up again | To kill the bad ones | So no one can find them
test: blackbird ate the rowan berries then pooed the seeds out on our car. gross. seeds dead? | No, they pass through unharmed and can still grow | Yes, the bird's tummy digests every seed | Yes, but only in winter | No, because they never went in, it spat them out
test: What do both the ant and the violet get from their deal? | The ant gets food, and the violet gets its seed carried to rich soil | The ant gets a home and the violet gets water | Only the ant gets anything | Only the violet gets anything
test: How might a seed that waits a long time help a plant survive bad years? | It can sprout later, when the drought or cold is over | It turns into a new kind of plant | It keeps the parent plant alive | It can't, waiting always kills seeds after a year or two in the soil
test: Is a banana in the shop full of real seeds like in old cartoons? | No, it has only tiny dark dots instead of real seeds | Yes, it has big hard seeds | Yes, and you must spit them all out like cherry stones | No, its seeds are in the skin
exam: Identify the means by which the seed of the dandelion is dispersed. | By wind, the descent being slowed by a parachute of fine hairs | By water, the seed being buoyant | By the adherence of small hooks to the fur of passing animals | By explosive rupture of a pod
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a fruit as defined by botanists? | The structure developed from the flower and enclosing the seeds | Any part of a plant that tastes sweet | Any edible part of a plant grown underground in the manner of the potato | A leaf modified for storage of sugar
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the dispersal of the coconut. | It is carried by the sea, the fibrous husk affording buoyancy | It is carried by birds that consume the entire fruit and its husk | It is flung explosively from the palm | It is buried by squirrels in the sand
exam: Ascertain the principal purpose of the Svalbard Global Seed Vault. | The preservation of seed samples against the loss of crops | The commercial sale of seeds to visitors | The cultivation of vegetables beneath the ice | The investigation of the effects of prolonged cold upon growth
